  "worked_solution": "We model the diameter of the balloon using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=-0.20D.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nD(t)={D}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.20t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${D}_{0}$ is the initial diameter.\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the diameter of the balloon is $15%$ of its original diameter. That is,\n\n$$\nD({t}_{1})=0.15{D}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve the above equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.15{D}_{0} & ={D}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.20{t}_{1}} \\\\ 0.15 & ={e}^{-0.20{t}_{1}} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.15) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{-0.20{t}_{1}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.15) & =-0.20{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.15)}{0.20} \\\\ {t}_{1} & \u22489.486\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding to the nearest second, we conclude that it takes $9$ seconds for the balloon\u2019s diameter to be $15%$ of its original diameter.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
